Recommended Architecture & Tech Stack
To keep the application fast and simple to maintain, build it as a Single Page Application (SPA) using HTML, CSS, JavaScript, and modern design standards:

Data Format (JSON or CSV): Convert your Excel spreadsheet into a .json file or export it as a .csv. JavaScript can parse this instantly without needing a back-end database.

Front-End Design (HTML/CSS): Use CSS Grid/Flexbox with media queries so the guide automatically scales from a multi-column desktop layout to a card-based view on mobile screens.

Date Picker & Filtering: A standard HTML <input type="date"> element combined with quick-filter buttons ("Today", "Saturday", "Show All").

Dynamic Color-Coding: Apply custom CSS classes based on your interest scale (e.g., tier/priority ranks):

Tier 1 (High Interest): Bold highlighted cards with vibrant accent borders/backgrounds (e.g., gold or bold primary colors) so marquee matchups stand out instantly.

Tier 2 (Moderate Interest): Muted background shading or subtle colored left borders to remain easily visible.

Tier 3 (Low Interest): Simple, clean black-and-white text on neutral backgrounds to avoid visual clutter.

Key Data Fields Needed from Your Excel File
When uploading or sharing your spreadsheet, ensure it includes:

Date & Time: Kickoff date and time (along with time zone, e.g., ET or CT).

Matchup: Away Team vs. Home Team (and ranking/seeds if applicable).

Network / Channel: Where to watch (ESPN, CBS, FOX, ABC, Streaming, etc.).

Interest Rating / Priority Tier: The numerical rank or category you use to color-code each game.

